ue | $136.2 | $106.0 | $136.9 | $146.7 |\n| Mine operating earnings/(loss) | $15.5 | ($0.3) | $17.9 | $1.1 |\n| Net earnings/(loss) | ($48.3) | ($13.6) | $10.2 | ($17.5) |\n\nSource: [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110]\n\n- **Revenue improved sequentially, not year over year:** Q2 revenue rose approximately **28% from Q1**, but remained **7% below Q2 2023** and slightly below Q4 2023. [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110]\n- **Mine-level performance strengthened:** earnings improved by approximately **$15.8 million from Q1** and **$14.3 million year over year**. However, the company still reported an overall **operating loss of $14.3 million**, versus $19.2 million in Q1 and $18.9 million a year earlier. Mine operating earnings should therefore not be confused with company-wide operating profit. [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71] [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.67]\n- **The bottom line deteriorated despite those gains:** the $48.3 million net loss was approximately **3.6 times Q1\u2019s loss** and **2.8 times the year-earlier loss**. The report principally attributes the year-over-year deterioration to a **$22.6 million non-cash deferred-tax expense**, versus a $10.3 million recovery, and an **$11.1 million foreign-exchange loss**, versus a $6.0 million gain. These outweighed the improvement in mine operating earnings. [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53]\n\n**In short:** better mine economics and a sequential sales rebound did not translate into net profitability, largely because of adverse tax and currency effects. [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53]", "expectedOutput": "In Q2 2024, revenues increased significantly to $136.2M (up 28% from Q1), driven by operational gains such as a 15% rise in ore processed and higher silver and gold production. However, Q2 also saw a much larger net loss of ($48.3M), up from ($13.6M) in Q1 and ($17.5M) in Q2 last year, indicating that despite improved operations and revenue, costs or other factors significantly impacted profitability compared to both prior quarter and the same quarter last year.", "context": ["2024 S