\\nCondensed Interim Consolidated Financial Statements Unaudited\\n]", "input": "If First Mint's Nevada facilities expand, how does PPE vs. derecognized mine asset classification shift in 2023?", "actualOutput": "If First Mint\u2019s Nevada facilities expanded in 2023, the expansion would remain classified as **property, plant and equipment (PPE)**\u2014for example, land/buildings, machinery, or assets under construction\u2014rather than as a derecognized mine asset. First Mint\u2019s Nevada bullion store and minting facility were reported within **\u201cOther\u201d PPE**, at approximately **$1.8 million in 2023**. [source FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.24]\n\nBy contrast, the derecognition applied to the **La Guitarra and La Parrilla mining assets**, which were held for sale and removed from the accounts after their 2023 dispositions. Their PPE components at disposal were **$4.003 million** and **$5.829 million**, respectively. [source FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.19]\n\nSo the shift would be:\n\n- **First Mint expansion:** increases PPE, potentially including assets under construction until placed in service.\n- **Disposed mine assets:** move from held-for-sale classification to derecognition upon sale; they no longer remain in PPE or mining interests.\n\nThe documents do not indicate that First Mint expansion itself caused any derecognition in 2023; it would be a capital-investment/PPE change, separate from the mine disposals.", "expectedOutput": "In 2023, First Mint\u2019s property, plant and equipment (PPE) in Nevada was included in \"Other\" at $51.8 million, but by 2024 this decreased to $3.1 million. Meanwhile, non-producing mines like La Guitarra and La Parrilla were classified as assets held-for-sale until their disposal, after which their assets and liabilities were derecognized as of December 31, 2023. Therefore, if First Mint\u2019s Nevada facilities expand, their PPE would increase and appear in \"Other,\" while derecognized mine assets would not be reflected on the balance sheet