 a shift from derecognition of sold mines to increased PPE from ongoing Nevada operations.-(3) Included in \"Other\" is property, plant and equipment of $3.1 million (2023 51.8 million) for First Mint which includes the Company's bullion store and its minting facility located in Nevada\\n\\nProperty, plant and equipment, including land and buildings, machinery and equipment; assets under construction and other assets above are allocated by mine as follow:\\n\\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Cognitactix-grupo-mexico-evals/dde52c23-b3a0-4425-a3ab-62404ca21536/images/1.png</parameter>\\n</invoke>\\nalt\\n\\n\\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Cognitactix-grupo-mexico-evals/dde52c23-b3a0-4425-a3ab-62404ca21536/images/1.png</parameter>\\n</invoke>\\nalt\\n\\nTabular amounts are expressed in thousands of US dollars)\\n\\nPage\\n\\nNOTES TO CONDENSED INTERIM CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 First Quarter Report\\n\\n(1) Non-producing properties include the San Martin, Del Toro, La Parrilla and La Guitarra mines. La Guitarra and La Parrilla were classified as assets held-for- sale up to the date of disposition on March 29, 2023 and August 14, 2023, respectively. As of December 31, 2023, the assets and liabilities have been derecognized\\n\\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of the condensed interim consolidated financial statements\\n\\nCondensed Interim Consolidated Financial Statements Unaudited\\n]", "input": "If First Mint's Nevada facilities expand, how does PPE vs. derecognized mine asset classification shift in 2023?", "actualOutput": "It does **not** look like a shift from PPE into \u201cderecognized mine assets.\u201d It is two separate classifications:\n\n- **First Mint / Nevada facility expansion \u2192 PPE.** First Mint is described as the company\u2019s operating minting facility in Nevada, using processing equipment to produce bullion products; expansion-related equipment or construction would stay in **property, plant and equipment (PPE)**\u2014potentially first as assets under construction, then reallocated within PPE when available for use [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.100; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.24]. In the 2023 comparative, First Mint\u2019s bullion store and Nevada minting facility were included in **\u201cOther\u201d PPE** [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.25]. From Jan. 1, 2024, First Mint became its own segment, with prior-period information re-presented from \u201cOthers\u201d into the First Mint segment\u2014but that is a segment presentation change, not a derecognition of assets [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.11].\n\n- **Derecognized mine assets in 2023 \u2192 La Guitarra and La Parrilla, not First Mint.** Those mines were held for sale until their 2023 dispositions and then derecognized [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.19; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.21]. At disposition, the removed balances included **mineral property interests** of $34.089m for La Guitarra and $13.891m for La Parrilla, plus **PPE** of $4.003m and $5.829m, respectively [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.43].\n\nSo, if First Mint\u2019s Nevada facilities expand, the accounting \u201cdirection\u201d is **more continuing PPE / First Mint or Other PPE**, while the 2023 derecognition bucket relates to **sold mining assets** being removed from the balance sheet\u2014not to First Mint.", "expectedOutput": "In 2023, First Mint\u2019s property, plant and equipment (PPE) in Nevada was included in \"Other\" at $51.8 million, but by 2024 this decreased to $3.1 million. Meanwhile, non-producing mines like La Guitarra and La Parrilla were classified as assets held-for-sale until their disposal, after which their assets and liabilities were derecognized as of December 31, 2023. Therefore, if First Mint\u2019s Nevada facilities expand, their PPE would increase and appear in \"Other,\" while derecognized mine assets would not be reflec