del": "gpt-4.1", "evaluationCost": 0.030984, "verboseLogs": "Criteria:\nEval\u00faa la PROFUNDIDAD ANAL\u00cdTICA de la respuesta del asistente: el nivel de an\u00e1lisis y la profundidad en la exploraci\u00f3n del tema.\n\nContexto recuperado (puede estar vac\u00edo; t\u00f3malo en cuenta solo si aporta informaci\u00f3n relevante para la evaluaci\u00f3n):\nse proporciona en el campo Retrieval Context.\n\nDevuelve la puntuaci\u00f3n y una justificaci\u00f3n breve en espa\u00f1ol. \n \nEvaluation Steps:\n[\n    \"Revisa el Input para identificar el tema y el nivel de complejidad que exige la pregunta o solicitud.\",\n    \"Examina el Actual Output para determinar si la respuesta aborda el tema con suficiente profundidad, ofrece an\u00e1lisis detallado y explora diferentes perspectivas o implicaciones.\",\n    \"Analiza el Retrieval Context (si est\u00e1 presente) y eval\u00faa en qu\u00e9 medida el Actual Output utiliza o integra informaci\u00f3n relevante de este contexto para reforzar su profundidad anal\u00edtica.\",\n    \"Comparte la puntuaci\u00f3n y una breve justificaci\u00f3n en espa\u00f1ol, fundamentando la decisi\u00f3n en el grado de an\u00e1lisis y profundidad alcanzado en relaci\u00f3n con el Input y el Retrieval Context.\"\n] \n \nRubric:\n0-2: An\u00e1lisis m\u00ednimo o inexistente; respuesta superficial\n3-4: An\u00e1lisis superficial; mayormente respuestas de nivel b\u00e1sico\n5-6: An\u00e1lisis moderado; algunos aspectos explorados superficialmente\n7-8: An\u00e1lisis s\u00f3lido con exploraci\u00f3n adecuada de aspectos clave\n9-10: An\u00e1lisis profundo y perspicaz; explora m\u00faltiples dimensiones del problema"}], "runDuration": 17.847286292002536, "evaluationCost": 0.378442}, {"name": "test_rag_quality[claude:claude-fable-5-1-How would a major asset sale in Q2 2024 affect assets, liabilities, and equity compared to prior periods?-A major asset sale in Q2 2024 would decrease total assets due to the removal of the sold asset. Depending on how the proceeds are used, liabilities may decrease if debts are paid off, or cash may increase if retained. Equity could increase if the asset is sold for more than its carrying value, resulting in a gain, or decrease in case of a loss. These changes would be reflected in the Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position as at June 30, 2024.-<invoke name=\"noop\">\\n</invoke>\\n\\nThe Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position provides a summary of assets, liabilities and equity, as vell as their current versus non-current nature as at the reporting date.\\n\\nAS AI JUNE 30, 2024 AND DECEMBER 31, 2023\\n\\nPage\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Second Quarter Report\\n\\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of the condensed interim consolidated financial statements\\n]", "input": "How would a major asset sale in Q2 2024 affect assets, liabilities, and equity compared to prior periods?", "actualOutput": "**Important caveat first:** the quarterly financial statements in this document set (FSMDA\u2011Q1/Q2/Q3\u20112024 and the 2024 annual report) are First Majestic Silver Corp.'s statements, not Grupo M\u00e9xico's [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.7; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.12]. Grupo M\u00e9xico's own 2024 sustainability report only gives aggregate figures (e.g., US$20,707 million of tangible assets, US$16,822 million of revenue) with no balance sheet or asset\u2011sale detail [IDS24_ESP_03_Valor_Compartido_ebook p.11, p.13]. Below I answer using the quarterly statements that are in the corpus.\n\n**1. There was no major asset sale in Q2 2024**\n\nThe Q2 2024 income statement shows \"Loss on sale of mining interest\" and \"Write\u2011down on asset held\u2011for\u2011sale\" at nil for both the three and six months ended June 30, 2024 (vs. $1.4 million and $7.2 million in the 2023 comparatives) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.4, p.98]. The company's divestitures actually closed in 2023: La Guitarra on March 29/30, 2023 and La Parrilla on August 14, 2023 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19, p.20; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.43]. So any Q2 2024 sale would be hypothetical.\n\n**2. Actual Q2 2024 balance sheet position (baseline)**\n\n- Equity: $1,374.0 million at June 30, 2024 vs. $1,358.1 million at Dec 31, 2023 (and $1,340.0 million at March 31, 2024) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.39; FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.7]\n- Debt facilities: $224.6 million vs. $219.8 million; lease liabilities $31.1 million vs. $36.7 million [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.29, p.32, p.39]\n- Cash: $152.2 million vs. $125.6 million [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.39]\n- Current assets $341.9 million / current liabilities $112.0 million \u2192 working capital $229.9 million (vs. $188.9 million at year\u2011end 2023) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.99]\n- Q2 2024 net loss: $48.3 million [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.98]\n\nTotal assets were roughly flat through 2024 ($1,955.7 million at Q1, $1,977.5 million at Q3, $1,979.8 million at year\u2011end vs. $1,976.4 million at Dec 2023) [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.7; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.7; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.11].\n\n**3. How a major sale would flow through, based on how the company accounted for its 2023 divestitures**\n\n- *Before closing (held\u2011for\u2011sale):* assets and liabilities of the disposal group are reclassified into current assets/current liabilities, measured at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell, and depreciation stops. If fair value is below carrying value, an impairment hits earnings and therefore equity \u2014 e.g., the $7.2 million write\u2011down on assets held\u2011for\u2011sale in Q2 2023 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.33; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.4].\n- *Assets:* on closing, the disposal group's assets are derecognized (La Guitarra and La Parrilla had net assets of $34.3 million and $14.9 million at disposal). Consideration is recognized in its place \u2014 in 2023 it was non\u2011cash (acquirer shares of $33.2 million and $13.8 million recorded as marketable securities at FVTOCI), so mining interests/PP&E fell while \"other financial assets\" rose; a cash sale would instead increase cash [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.20; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.43].\n- *Liabilities:* liabilities attached to the sold asset leave the balance sheet when the sale is a share deal (e.g., $10.1 million and $6.4 million of leases, deferred tax and decommissioning liabilities for the two 2023 sales). Note the exception: because La Parrilla was an asset purchase agreement, its liabilities remained 100% with the company [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.20; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.76]. Debt facilities and lease liabilities would only change if proceeds were used to repay them.\n- *Equity:* moves by the gain or loss on disposal (consideration minus net carrying value) \u2014 losses of $1.4 million and $1.6 million in 2023 \u2014 plus any prior held\u2011for\u2011sale write\u2011down; subsequent fair\u2011value changes in shares received go through OCI reserves [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.20, p.98; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.12].\n\nIn short, relative to the Q1 2024 and Dec 2023 balance sheets, a large Q2 2024 sale would have shifted the asset mix (non\u2011current mining assets \u2192 cash or securities), removed the related liabilities only if structured as a share sale, and changed equity by the net gain/loss rather than by the gross proceeds. The documents contain no such transaction for Q2 2024, so no actual impact figures can be cited.", "expectedOutput": "A major asset sale in Q2 2024 would decrease total assets due to the removal of the sold asset. Depending on how the proceeds are used, liabilities may decrease if debts are paid off, or cash may increase if retained. Equity could increase if the asset is sold for more than its carrying value, resulting in a gain, or decrease in case of a loss. These changes would be reflected in the Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position as at June 30, 2024.", "context": ["<invoke name=\"noop\">\n</invoke>\n\nThe Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position provides a summary of assets, liabilities and equity, as vell as t