.5 | $77.2 | (27%) |\\n| Cash and Cash Equivalents | $152.2 | $102.1 | 49% | $160.2 | (5%) | $152.2 | $160.2 | (5%) |\\n| Total Assets | $1,987.0 | $1,955.7 | 2% | $2,014.0 | (1%) | $1,987.0 | $2,014.0 | (1%) |\\n| Total Non-Current Financial Liabilities | $501.1 | $495.9 | 1% | $512.1 | (2%) | $501.1 | $512.1 | (2%) |\\n| Working Capital\\u207d\\xb9\\u207e | $229.9 | $159.6 | 44% | $237.2 | (3%) | $229.9 | $237.2 | (3%) |\\n| Free Cash Flow\\u207d\\xb9\\u207e | $6.4 | $0.9 | NM | $7.4 | (13%) | $7.4 | ($19.3) | 138% |\\n| | | | | | | | | |\\n| **Shareholders** | | |]", "input": "How do Q2 revenue and operational gains compare with Q2 net loss and prior quarters\u2019 figures?", "actualOutput": "**A note on the source first:** the quarterly financial filings in this document set (the \"FSMDA\" files) are the MD&A of **First Majestic Silver Corp.** (San Dimas, Santa Elena, Jerritt Canyon) \u2014 the Q2 report is explicitly titled \"First Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Second Quarter Report\" [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.52]. Grupo M\u00e9xico's own sustainability report only gives annual, division-level figures (e.g., consolidated 2024 revenue of US$16,169 million and pre-tax profit of US$6,603 million) [IDS24_ESP_03_Valor_Compartido_ebook p.13], with no quarterly breakdown. So the Q2 comparison below is based on the First Majestic filings.\n\n## Q2 2024 at a glance (US$)\n\n| | Q2 2024 | Q1 2024 | Q2 2023 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | $136.2M | $106.0M | $146.7M |\n| Mine operating earnings (loss) | $15.5M | ($0.3M) | $1.1M |\n| Operating loss | ($14.3M) | ($19.2M) | ($18.9M) |\n| Net loss | ($48.3M) | ($13.6M) | ($17.5M) |\n| EPS | ($0.17) | ($0.05) | ($0.06) |\n\n[FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71; FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.67; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110]\n\n## Revenue and operating performance\n\n- **Revenue** of $136.2M was down 7% year-over-year, due to a 15% drop in payable silver-equivalent ounces sold (higher quarter-end inventory, lower output at San Dimas amid labour negotiations, and the loss of $15.7M of Jerritt Canyon revenue that existed in Q2 2023). This was partly offset by an 11% higher realized silver price ($27.81 vs $24.95/oz), which alone added $10.7M of revenue [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53, p.71].\n- Sequentially, however, revenue jumped about 28% from Q1 2024's $106.0M [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110].\n- **Mine operating earnings** rose to $15.5M from a $0.3M loss in Q1, driven by more ounces sold and a higher silver price, partly offset by higher cash cost per ounce [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.76]. Versus Q2 2023, mine operating earnings improved by $14.3M, helped by a 15% cut in cost of sales and the elimination of $8.4M of standby costs [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53, p.71].\n- The **operating loss** narrowed to $14.3M from $18.9M a year earlier, despite an $11.1M foreign-exchange loss (vs a $6.0M gain in Q2 2023) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71].\n\n## Why the net loss widened anyway\n\nThe $48.3M net loss was nearly triple Q2 2023's $17.5M and more than triple Q1 2024's $13.6M \u2014 even though operations improved. The gap is almost entirely non-cash items below the operating line:\n- a $22.6M **deferred income tax expense** (vs a $10.3M tax recovery in Q2 2023), and\n- the $11.1M **non-cash FX loss** (vs a $6.0M gain) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53].\n\nStripping out such items, the adjusted net loss was $20.4M (($0.07)/share) vs $5.5M in Q2 2023 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53]. Operating cash flow before working-capital changes and taxes was $23.8M vs $26.9M a year earlier [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53].\n\n## Wider quarterly context\n\nQ2 2024 was the trough for net results in 2024 but the start of an operating recovery. Q1 2024 had seen revenue fall and mine operating earnings turn negative on 21% fewer ounces sold and a strong peso [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.69]. After Q2, the trend improved: Q3 revenue $146.1M, mine operating earnings $28.5M, net loss $26.6M; Q4 revenue $172.3M, mine operating earnings $48.2M, net loss $13.5M [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110]. In both later quarters, net losses again reflected non-cash deferred tax and FX charges rather than operating weakness [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.55; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.87]. For comparison, 2023 quarterly net results were ($100.7M), ($17.5M), ($27.1M) and +$10.2M [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110].\n\n**Bottom line:** In Q2 2024, revenue and mine operating earnings improved sharply over Q1 2024 and operating earnings improved over Q2 2023 (revenue was slightly lower), but the net loss widened considerably because of non-cash deferred tax and foreign-exchange charges, not because of weaker operations.", "expectedOutput": "In Q2 2024, revenues increased significantly to $136.2M (up 28% from Q1), driven by operational gains such as a 15% rise in ore processed and higher silver and gold production. However, Q2 also saw a much larger net loss of ($48.3M), up from ($13.6M) in Q1 and ($17.5M) in Q2 last year, indicating that despite improved operations and revenue, costs or other factors significantly impacted profitability compared to both prior quarter and the same quarter last year.", "context": ["2024 SECOND QUARTER HIGHLIGHTS\n\n| Key Performance Metrics | 2024-Q2 | 2024-Q1 | Change Q2 vs Q1 | 2023-Q2 | Change Q2 vs Q2 | 2024-YTD | 2023-YTD | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Operational** | | | | | | | | |\n| Ore Processed / Tonnes Milled | 674,570 | 588,651 | 15% | 733,170 | (8%) | 1,263,221 | 1,579,038 | (20%) |\n| Silver Ounces Produced | 2,104,181 | 1,975,176 | 7% | 2,633,411 | (20%) | 4,079,358 | 5,176,470 | (21%) |\n| Gold Ounces Produced | 39,339 | 35,936 | 9% | 45,022 | (13%) | 75,275 | 105,616 | (29%) |\n| Silver Equivalent Ounces Produced | 5,289,439 | 5,162,283 | 2% | 6,320,971 | (16%) | 10,451,724 | 13,948,076 | (25%) |\n| Cash Costs per Silver Equivalent Ounce\u207d\u00b9\u207e | $15.29 | $15.00 | 2% | $15.58 | (2%) | $15.14 | $15.35 | (1%) |\n| All-in Sustaining Cost per Silver Equivalent Ounce\u207d\u00b9\u207e | $21.64 | $21.53 | 1% | $21.52 | 1% | $21.58 | $21.17 | 2% |\n| Total Production Cost per Tonne\u207d\u00b9\u207e | $113.16