on shift in 2023?", "actualOutput": "**Short answer: the documents don't support this question as framed \u2014 \"First Mint\" is not a Grupo Mexico entity, and nothing in the retrieved material describes a 2023 reclassification between PPE and derecognized mine assets tied to a Nevada expansion.**\n\nHere is what the search actually turned up:\n\n**1. First Mint belongs to First Majestic Silver Corp., not Grupo Mexico.**\nThe only hits for \"First Mint\" come from First Majestic Silver Corp. quarterly/annual reports, which describe First Mint, LLC as First Majestic's 100%-owned minting facility in Nevada that commenced commissioning in March 2024 and was inaugurated on September 26, 2024 [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.62; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.54; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.100]. Grupo Mexico's own reports show its U.S. mining footprint as ASARCO in Arizona (Silver Bell, Ray, Hayden, Mission) \u2014 no Nevada facilities appear anywhere [IDS24_ESP_05_Social_ebook p.86].\n\n**2. No \"derecognized mine asset\" classification shift in 2023 is documented.**\nThe closest accounting disclosure in the First Majestic material is:\n- A general PPE policy: assets under construction are recorded at cost and re-allocated to land and buildings, machinery and equipment, or other when they become available for use; depreciation begins once management's intended operating levels are reached [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.23; p.47].\n- A 2023 **impairment** (not a derecognition) of non-current assets totaling $125.2 million \u2014 $78.1 million on producing mining interests, $17.4 million on non-depletable exploration properties, and $29.7 million on PPE \u2014 net $94.0 million after a $31.2 million deferred tax recovery [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.50]. The passage does not attribute this to First Mint; the same reports note Jerritt Canyon (Nevada) was placed on temporary suspension in March 2023 [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.12], which is a more plausible context, but the documents don't explicitly link the two.\n\n**3. The hypothetical (\"if First Mint's Nevada facilities expand\") has no documented answer.**\nNone of the retrieved passages model how a future expansion would move amounts between PPE and derecognized assets. In 2023, First Mint's results were in fact still reported inside the \"Others\" segment and only became a separate segment in 2024 [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.12].\n\nIf you intended to ask about Grupo Mexico's PPE accounting (e.g., ASARCO or Southern Copper asset write-offs/derecognition in 2023), or about First Majestic's Jerritt Canyon impairment specifically, let me know and I can search for that directly.", "expectedOutput": "In 2023, First Mint\u2019s property, plant and equipment (PPE) in Nevada was included in \"Other\" at $51.8 million, but by 2024 this decreased to $3.1 million. Meanwhile, non-producing mines like La Guitarra and La Parrilla were classified as assets held-for-sale until their disposal, after which their assets and liabilities were derecognized as of December 31, 2023. Therefore, if First Mint\u2019s Nevada facilities expand, their PPE would increase and appear in \"Other,\" while derecognized mine assets would not be reflected on the balance sheet, demonstrating a shift from derecognition of sold mines to increased PPE from ongoing Nevada operations.", "context": ["(3) Included in \"Other\" is property, plant and equipment of $3.1 million (2023 51.8 million) for First Mint which includes the Company's bullion store and its minting facility located in Nevada\n\nProperty, plant and equipment, including land and buildings, machinery and equipment; assets under construction and other assets above are allocated by mine as follow:\n\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Cognitactix-grupo-mexico-evals/dde52c23-b3a0-4425-a3ab-62404ca21536/images/1.png</parameter>\n</invoke>\nalt\n\n\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Co