fundo y perspicaz; explora m\u00faltiples dimensiones del problema"}], "runDuration": 17.876140584005043, "evaluationCost": 0.353834}, {"name": "test_rag_quality[claude:claude-opus-5-5-How would a major asset sale in Q2 2024 affect assets, liabilities, and equity compared to prior periods?-A major asset sale in Q2 2024 would decrease total assets due to the removal of the sold asset. Depending on how the proceeds are used, liabilities may decrease if debts are paid off, or cash may increase if retained. Equity could increase if the asset is sold for more than its carrying value, resulting in a gain, or decrease in case of a loss. These changes would be reflected in the Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position as at June 30, 2024.-<invoke name=\"noop\">\\n</invoke>\\n\\nThe Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position provides a summary of assets, liabilities and equity, as vell as their current versus non-current nature as at the reporting date.\\n\\nAS AI JUNE 30, 2024 AND DECEMBER 31, 2023\\n\\nPage\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Second Quarter Report\\n\\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of the condensed interim consolidated financial statements\\n]", "input": "How would a major asset sale in Q2 2024 affect assets, liabilities, and equity compared to prior periods?", "actualOutput": "**I couldn't find a major asset sale in Q2 2024 in these documents, so I can't measure the effect of one.** There's also a bigger problem: the quarterly 2024 statements that came back aren't Grupo Mexico's. They're First Majestic Silver Corp.'s (it names itself in the 2024 Annual Report, and its mines are San Dimas, La Encantada and Santa Elena) [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.12]. I didn't find any Grupo Mexico balance-sheet figures for Q2 2024. Everything below is about First Majestic, so please check that it's useful before relying on it.\n\n**What changed in Q2 2024 (June 30, 2024 vs. Dec 31, 2023, US$ thousands)**\n- Current assets went from 309,057 to 341,903. Current liabilities fell from 120,138 to 111,965. Working capital therefore rose from 188,919 to 229,938 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.99].\n- Property, plant and equipment fell from 406,294 to 390,801. That's mostly depreciation, plus about 2.5 million in disposals and transfers, which is not a major sale [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.24].\n- Debt rose from 219,812 to 224,577. Lease liabilities fell from 36,702 to 31,090 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.29, p.32].\n- Equity: the search didn't return a June 30 total. Equity was 1,358,120 at Dec 2023, 1,339,954 at March 2024 and 1,377,310 at September 2024 [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.7; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.7]. Most of the 2024 increase in share capital came from selling new shares, not assets. Through September 2024, 14.3 million shares were sold for net proceeds of $93.9 million [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.75].\n\n**How a sale actually showed up: the 2023 example**\nThe documents' real divestitures happened in 2023: La Guitarra (March 2023) and La Parrilla (August 2023) [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.19]. They show how a sale moves through the balance sheet:\n1. **Before closing:** once the sale was \"highly probable,\" the related assets and liabilities were moved into separate \"held for sale\" lines under current assets and current liabilities [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19].\n2. **At closing:** those assets and liabilities were removed from the books [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.19].\n3. **Equity effect:** the price is compared with the book value. La Guitarra sold for $33.2 million (paid in Sierra Madre shares) against a book value of $34.3 million. After a $0.2 million working-capital adjustment, the result was a $1.4 million loss, which reduces equity through the accumulated deficit [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.68].\n4. **Asset mix:** because the buyer paid in shares, total assets barely changed. The mine was replaced by a financial investment, and later changes in its value go through other comprehensive income [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.68].\n\nIn general, a sale paid in cash would lower long-term asset