equity compared to prior periods?-A major asset sale in Q2 2024 would decrease total assets due to the removal of the sold asset. Depending on how the proceeds are used, liabilities may decrease if debts are paid off, or cash may increase if retained. Equity could increase if the asset is sold for more than its carrying value, resulting in a gain, or decrease in case of a loss. These changes would be reflected in the Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position as at June 30, 2024.-<invoke name=\"noop\">\\n</invoke>\\n\\nThe Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position provides a summary of assets, liabilities and equity, as vell as their current versus non-current nature as at the reporting date.\\n\\nAS AI JUNE 30, 2024 AND DECEMBER 31, 2023\\n\\nPage\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 Second Quarter Report\\n\\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of the condensed interim consolidated financial statements\\n]", "input": "How would a major asset sale in Q2 2024 affect assets, liabilities, and equity compared to prior periods?", "actualOutput": "## Short answer first\n\n**The documents record no major asset sale in Q2 2024.** The two large divestitures disclosed (La Guitarra and La Parrilla) both closed in **2023** (March 29, 2023 and August 14, 2023), and the FY2024 income statement shows \"Loss on sale of mining interest\" of **$0 in 2024 vs. $3,024k in 2023** [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.8, p.42-43]. So the question has to be answered as a **scenario**, using the accounting treatment and the actual Q2 2024 balance-sheet base the filings provide.\n\n*(Note: the quarterly financial statements in this document set are those of First Majestic Silver Corp., not Grupo M\u00e9xico's own consolidated accounts.)*\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Q2 2024 starting point\n\n| Item | June 30, 2024 | Dec 31, 2023 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current assets | $341,903k | $309,057k |\n| Current liabilities | $(111,965)k | $(120,138)k |\n| **Working capital** | **$229,938k** | $188,919k |\n| Equity | $1,374,013k | $1,358,120k |\n| Debt facilities | $224,577k | $219,812k |\n| Lease liabilities | $31,090k | $36,702k |\n| Cash | $(152,173)k | $(125,581)k |\n\n[FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.39, p.99]\n\nSurrounding quarters for comparison: Q1-24 assets $1,955,708k / liabilities $615,754k / equity $1,339,954k; Q3-24 $1,977,495k / $600,185k / $1,377,310k; Q4-24 $1,979,788k / $628,717k / $1,351,071k [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.7; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.7; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.11].\n\n## 2. What a sale would do, step by step (per the company's own policy)\n\n**(a) Before closing \u2014 held-for-sale reclassification.** Once a sale is \"highly probable,\" the assets *and* the related liabilities are moved out of non-current and shown separately **inside current assets and current liabilities**; they are measured at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell, and **depreciation/depletion stops** [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.33, p.42]. Effect in Q2 2024: total assets and total liabilities would be unchanged in aggregate, but **current assets and current liabilities would jump**, distorting the working-capital trend (the reported $229.9m). If FVLCTS were below carrying value, an impairment hits earnings \u2014 exactly what happened in Q2 2023, when a **$7,229k \"write down on assets held-for-sale\"** was booked [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71].\n\n**(b) At closing \u2014 derecognition.** Assets and liabilities are removed from the balance sheet. The La Guitarra template: gross assets less **total liabilities of $10,098k** (leases $21k, deferred tax liabilities $6,894k, decommissioning $2,951k) gave **net assets disposed of $34,337k**; consideration was **$33,172k entirely in shares**, producing a **$1,378k loss on disposal** [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.20; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19].\n\nSo the three lines move as follows:\n\n- **Assets:** fall by the carrying value of the disposed mining interests/PP&E, but rise again by the consideration received. In the 2023 deals the consideration was **non-cash (Sierra Madre / Silver Storm shares)**, so the effect was largely a **reclassification within assets** \u2014 mining interests/PP&E \u2192 \"Other financial assets / marketable securities\" at FVTOCI \u2014 with almost no change in total assets [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.43]. A **cash** sale would instead raise cash (and working capital/liquidity) one-for-one.\n- **Liabilities:** fall by the liabilities that travel with the asset \u2014 decommissioning provisions, lease liabilities and deferred tax. This is material in this balance sheet: decommissioning alone was $151.6m\u2013$159.1m and deferred tax liabilities ~$79\u201381m across 2024 [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.7; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.11]. Debt facilities ($224.6m at June 30, 2024) would be unaffected unless proceeds were used to repay them.\n- **Equity:** changes **only by the gain/loss net of tax**, flowing through accumulated deficit \u2014 i.e. the smallest of the three movements. La Guitarra moved equity by just $1.4m on a $34m asset [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.19]. If paid in shares held at FVTOCI, subsequent value changes land in **equity reserves/OCI**, not earnings \u2014 note equity reserves swung from $88,025k (Dec-23) to $102,745k (Q3-24) and back to $90,028k (Q4-24) [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.7; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.11].\n\n## 3. Sizing \"major\" against the asset base\n\nSegment carrying values at March 31, 2024 give a sense of scale (total assets / total liabilities): San Dimas $585,422k / $91,346k; Santa Elena $362,801k / $90,204k; La Encantada $109,781k / $26,293k; non-producing properties $142,978k / $15,844k [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.13]. Selling a core mine at carrying value would cut total assets by roughly 18\u201330% from the ~$1.96bn Q2 level and reduce liabilities by $90m+ (\u224815% of the ~$600\u2013620m total), while equity would barely move unless the price diverged materially from book \u2014 which is why a sale materially **shrinks the balance sheet and lowers leverage without creating equity**.\n\n## 4. Comparability with prior periods\n\nThree caveats the filings make visible:\n- **Balance-sheet comparatives are not restated**, so the Dec 31, 2023 column would still include the sold asset \u2014 Q2-vs-prior-period deltas would overstate \"deterioration\" in mining interests and PP&E.\n- **Revenue and operating metrics lose like-for-like comparability**; the 2023 divestitures contributed to the FY2024 revenue decline to $560,604k from $573,801k [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.8].\n- **Liquidity ratios improve mechanically** if consideration is cash; the 2023 share-based structure did *not* improve cash \u2014 the 61% cash increase to $202.2m in 2024 was attributed to higher mine operating earnings, not disposals [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.83].\n\n**Bottom line:** a major Q2 2024 sale would have shrunk both sides of the balance sheet \u2014 assets down by the disposed carrying value (offset if paid in shares), liabilities down via transferred decommissioning, lease and deferred-tax obligations \u2014 while equity would have moved only by a small disposal gain/loss plus OCI. That is a scenario inference from documented policy and the 2023 precedent, not a disclosed 2024 transaction.", "expectedOutput": "A major asset sale in Q2 2024 would decrease total assets due to the removal of the sold asset. Depending on how the proceeds are used, liabilities may decrease if debts are paid off, or cash may increase if retained. Equity could increase if the asset is sold for more than its carrying value, resulting in a gain, or decrease in case of a loss. These changes would be reflected in the Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position as at June 30, 2024.", "context": ["<invoke name=\"noop\">\n</invoke>\n\nThe Condensed Interim Consolidated Statements of Financial Position provides a summary of assets, liabilities and equity, as vell as their current versus non-current nature as at the reporting date.\n\nAS AI JUNE 30, 2024 AND DECEMBER 31, 2023\n\nPage\n\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 