692 |\n| Mine operating earnings (loss) | (321) | **15,462** | 28,522 | 48,237 | 1,138 |\n| Net (loss) earnings after tax | (13,563) | **(48,251)** | (26,593) | (13,478) | (17,534) |\n| EPS basic | (0.05) | **(0.17)** | (0.09) | (0.04) | (0.06) |\n\n[2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110]\n\n**Revenue:** Q2 revenue of $136.2M was ~28% above Q1's $106.0M, but 7% *below* the $146.7M of Q2 2023. The year-over-year decline came from a 15% drop in payable AgEq ounces sold (higher quarter-end inventories, lower San Dimas output amid labour negotiations, and the loss of $15.7M of Jerritt Canyon revenue), only partly offset by an 11% higher realized silver price of $27.81/oz vs $24.95/oz, worth about +$10.7M [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53, p.71].\n\n**Operational gains:** Mine operating earnings swung to $15.5M from a $0.3M loss in Q1 (more AgEq ounces sold and a higher realized price) and jumped from just $1.1M in Q2 2023, helped by cost of sales falling 15% and the elimination of $8.4M of standby costs [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71, p.76]. Even so, at the operating line the company still posted a **$14.3M operating loss**, albeit 24% better than the $18.9M operating loss of Q2 2023 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71].\n\n**Net loss:** Despite the operating improvement, the net loss widened to **$48.3M (EPS \u2011$0.17)** \u2014 the largest of 2024 and nearly four times the $13.6M of Q1 and almost triple the $17.5M of Q2 2023. The gap is explained almost entirely by non-cash items:\n- a **$22.6M deferred income tax expense**, versus a $10.3M recovery in Q2 2023 and a $10.7M recovery in Q1 2024;\n- a **$11.1M foreign-exchange loss**, versus a $6.0M gain in Q2 2023 and a $1.2M gain in Q1 2024 \u2014 both tied to peso movements;\n- partly offset by the $14.3M increase in mine operating earnings year over year [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53, p.72, p.76].\n\nStripping those items out, **adjusted net loss was $20.4M (adjusted EPS \u2011$0.07)**, versus $5.5M in Q2 2023 and $18.4M in Q1 2024 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53; FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.52]. Operating cash flow before working capital and taxes was $23.8M, slightly below $26.9M in Q2 2023 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53].\n\n## How it fits in the sequence\nQ2 was the inflection point of a clear operational recovery through 2024 \u2014 mine operating earnings rose from \u2011$0.3M (Q1) to $15.5M (Q2), $28.5M (Q3) and $48.2M (Q4), with revenue climbing each quarter to $172.3M in Q4 \u2014 while reported net losses narrowed after the Q2 spike, to $26.6M in Q3 and $13.5M in Q4 [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110, p.87; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.55]. In short, Q2's headline loss was driven by deferred tax and FX non-cash charges rather than by the underlying operations, which were improving both sequentially and year over year. (Adjusted EBITDA is not available for Q2, as the company only began reporting it in Q3 2024 \u2014 $39.8M in Q3 and $64.8M in Q4 [FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.99, p.54; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.87].)", "expectedOutput": "In Q2 2024, revenues increased significantly to $136.2M (up 28% from Q1), driven by operational gains such as a 15% rise in ore processed and higher silver and gold production. However, Q2 also saw a much larger net loss of ($48.3M), up from ($13.6M) in Q1 and ($17.5M) in Q2 last year, indicating that despite improved operations and revenue, costs or other factors significantly impacted profitability compared to both prior quarter and the same quarter last year.", "context": ["2024 SECOND QUARTER HIGHLIGHTS\n\n| Key Performance Metrics | 2024-Q2 | 2024-Q1 | Change Q2 vs Q1 | 2023-Q2 | Change Q2 vs Q2 | 2024-YTD | 2023-YTD | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Operational** | | | | | | | | |\n| Ore Processed / Tonnes Milled | 674,570 | 588,651 | 15% | 733,170 | (8%) | 1,263,221 | 1,579,038 | (20%) |\n| Silver Ounces Produced | 2,104,181 | 1,975,176 | 7% | 2,633,411 | (20%) | 4,079,358 | 5,176,470 | (21%) |\n| Gold Ounces Produced | 39,339 | 35,936 | 9% | 45,022 | (13%) | 75,275 | 105,616 | (29%) |\n| Silver Equivalent Ounces Produced | 5,289,439 | 5,162,283 | 2% | 6,3