\n| Q1 2024 | $106,014K |\n| Q4 2023 | $136,946K |\n| Q3 2023 | $133,211K |\n| Q2 2023 | $146,692K |\n| Q1 2023 | $156,952K |\n\nQ2 2024 revenue of **$136.2M** was **7% lower than Q2 2023** ($146.7M), but meaningfully **higher than Q1 2024** ($106.0M), representing a sequential recovery [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71], [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110].\n\n---\n\n### 2. \u26cf\ufe0f Operational (Mine Operating) Earnings\n\n| Quarter | Mine Operating Earnings/(Loss) |\n|---|---|\n| **Q2 2024** | **$15,462K** |\n| Q1 2024 | ($321K) \u2014 a loss |\n| Q4 2023 | $17,901K |\n| Q3 2023 | $13,026K |\n| Q2 2023 | $1,138K |\n| Q1 2023 | ($6,423K) \u2014 a loss |\n\nQ2 2024 mine operating earnings of **$15.5M** were a dramatic turnaround \u2014 reversing the Q1 2024 operating loss of ($0.3M) and vastly outperforming Q2 2023's near-breakeven $1.1M. This improvement was driven by an increase in payable silver-equivalent ounces sold, a higher average realized silver price, and a significant **17% reduction in total mine operating costs** (cost of sales fell 15%; standby costs were eliminated entirely vs. $8.4M in Q2 2023) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71], [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.76].\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udcc9 Net Loss: A Widening Gap\n\nDespite operational improvement, the **net loss widened sharply**:\n\n| Quarter | Net (Loss)/Earnings | EPS (Basic) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Q2 2024** | **($48,251K)** | **($0.17)** |\n| Q1 2024 | ($13,563K) | ($0.05) |\n| Q4 2023 | $10,231K | $0.04 |\n| Q3 2023 | ($27,149K) | ($0.09) |\n| Q2 2023 | ($17,534K) | ($0.06) |\n| Q1 2023 | ($100,660K) | ($0.37) |\n\nQ2 2024 posted the **largest net loss in the prior 8-quarter period** outside of the exceptional Q1 2023 (which included a $125.2M Jerritt Canyon impairment charge). The Q2 2024 net loss of **$48.3M** was nearly **3.5\u00d7 worse than Q1 2024** ($13.6M) [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53], [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110].\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udd0d Why the Disconnect Between Operational Gains and Net Loss?\n\nThe key drivers of the outsized net loss \u2014 despite strong mine operating earnings \u2014 were **non-cash, below-the-line items** [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53]:\n\n- **Non-cash deferred income tax expense: $22.6M** (vs. a $10.3M *recovery* in Q2 2023 \u2014 a ~$32.9M swing)\n- **Non-cash foreign exchange loss: $11.1M** (vs. a $6.0M *gain* in Q2 2023 \u2014 a ~$17.1M swing)\n- The operating loss itself narrowed to **($14.3M)** from ($18.9M) in Q2 2023, a **24% improvement** [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71]\n- **Adjusted net loss** (stripping out these non-cash items) was **$20.4M** (EPS: -$0.07), worsened from $5.5M (EPS: -$0.02) in Q2 2023 [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53]\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n> **Q2 2024 presented a tale of two stories**: operationally, the company was recovering strongly \u2014 revenue rebounded from Q1 2024 and mine operating earnings surged to $15.5M. But at the net income level, the quarter was the worst in two years, driven entirely by large non-cash charges (deferred tax expense and foreign exchange losses) rather than underlying business performance [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.53], [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.71], [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.110].", "expectedOutput": "In Q2 2024, revenues increased significantly to $136.2M (up 28% from Q1), driven by operational gains such as a 15% rise in ore processed and higher silver and gold production. However, Q2 also saw a much larger net loss of ($48.3M), up from ($13.6M) in Q1 and ($17.5M) in Q2 last year, indicating that despite improved operations and revenue, costs or other factors significantly impacted profitability compared to both prior quarter and the same quarter last year.", "context": ["2024 SECOND QUARTER HIGHLIGHTS\n\n| Key Performance Metrics | 2024-Q2 | 2024-Q1 | Change Q2 vs Q1 | 2023-Q2 | Change Q2 vs Q2 | 2024-YTD | 2023-YTD | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Operational** | | | | | | | | |\n| Ore Processed / Tonnes Milled | 674,570 | 588,651 | 15% | 733,170 | (8%) | 1,263,221 | 1,579,038 | (20%) |\n| Silver Ounces Produced | 2,104,181 | 1,975,176 | 7% | 2,