ny's bullion store and its minting facility located in Nevada\\n\\nProperty, plant and equipment, including land and buildings, machinery and equipment; assets under construction and other assets above are allocated by mine as follow:\\n\\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Cognitactix-grupo-mexico-evals/dde52c23-b3a0-4425-a3ab-62404ca21536/images/1.png</parameter>\\n</invoke>\\nalt\\n\\n\\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Cognitactix-grupo-mexico-evals/dde52c23-b3a0-4425-a3ab-62404ca21536/images/1.png</parameter>\\n</invoke>\\nalt\\n\\nTabular amounts are expressed in thousands of US dollars)\\n\\nPage\\n\\nNOTES TO CONDENSED INTERIM CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\\n\\nFirst Majestic Silver Corp. 2024 First Quarter Report\\n\\n(1) Non-producing properties include the San Martin, Del Toro, La Parrilla and La Guitarra mines. La Guitarra and La Parrilla were classified as assets held-for- sale up to the date of disposition on March 29, 2023 and August 14, 2023, respectively. As of December 31, 2023, the assets and liabilities have been derecognized\\n\\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of the condensed interim consolidated financial statements\\n\\nCondensed Interim Consolidated Financial Statements Unaudited\\n]", "input": "If First Mint's Nevada facilities expand, how does PPE vs. derecognized mine asset classification shift in 2023?", "actualOutput": "Based on thorough searching across all available documents, I need to be transparent with you about several important issues with your question:\n\n---\n\n## Important Clarifications\n\n### 1. \"First Mint\" is **not a Grupo M\u00e9xico entity**\n\nThe documents reveal that **First Mint, LLC** is a 100%-owned subsidiary of **First Majestic Silver Corp.** \u2014 a Canadian silver mining company \u2014 not Grupo M\u00e9xico. First Mint is a minting and bullion facility located in Nevada, USA [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.62; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.100]. Grupo M\u00e9xico's U.S. operations are conducted through **ASARCO**, which has facilities in Arizona, not Nevada [IDS24_ESP_06_Ambiental_ebook p.157].\n\n### 2. The PPE vs. derecognized mine asset question in 2023 involves **two separate, unrelated dynamics**\n\nThe documents do describe both topics, but they are **not linked to a First Mint expansion**:\n\n#### A. First Mint's PPE (First Majestic Silver, not Grupo M\u00e9xico)\nFirst Mint's Nevada facility was **under construction/commissioning** in 2023, and its PPE was reported within the **\"Other\" segment** of First Majestic's consolidated PP&E schedule at **$1.8 million at December 31, 2023**, rising to $3.1M (Q1-2024), $3.4M (Q2-2024), and $4.1M (Q3-2024) as new equipment (coin presses, lasers, etching tools) was commissioned [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.24; FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.25; FSMDA-Q3-2024 p.25]. Any expansion would increase this \"Other\" PPE bucket \u2014 but this pertains to **First Majestic**, not Grupo M\u00e9xico.\n\n#### B. Derecognized Mine Assets in 2023 (First Majestic Silver)\nThe derecognitions in 2023 relate entirely to the **sale of the La Guitarra and La Parrilla silver mines in Mexico** \u2014 not Nevada:\n- **La Guitarra** was sold to Sierra Madre Gold and Silver Ltd. on **March 29, 2023**, and its assets/liabilities were fully derecognized [FSMDA-Q1-2024 p.20].\n- **La Parrilla** was sold to Silver Storm Mining Ltd. on **August 14, 2023**, and its assets/liabilities were similarly derecognized [FSMDA-Q2-2024 p.21; 2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.76].\n\nThe overall PP&E cost schedule shows the net effect of these disposals: total PP&E cost moved from **$942.9M (Dec 31, 2022)** to **$973.5M (Dec 31, 2023)**, with a reclassification to assets held-for-sale of only $12K, and transfers/disposals of **\u2013$6.6M** [2024-Q4-FS-MDA p.47].\n\n---\n\n### Bottom Line\n\nThe documents **do not contain any scenario** in which a First Mint Nevada expansion causes a shift in PPE vs. derecognized mine asset classification, because:\n1. First Mint belongs to **First Majestic Silver**, not Grupo M\u00e9xico.\n2. The 2023 derecognitions were driven by **Mexican mine divestitures** (La Guitarra, La Parrilla), entirely separate from any Nevada activity.\n3. No hypothetical expansion scenario for First Mint's Nevada facility and its accounting classification impact is discussed in any of the available documents.\n\nIf you're interested in either **First Majestic's First Mint PPE trajectory** or **Grupo M\u00e9xico's ASARCO U.S. asset classification**, I'd be happy to search further on those specific topics.", "expectedOutput": "In 2023, First Mint\u2019s property, plant and equipment (PPE) in Nevada was included in \"Other\" at $51.8 million, but by 2024 this decreased to $3.1 million. Meanwhile, non-producing mines like La Guitarra and La Parrilla were classified as assets held-for-sale until their disposal, after which their assets and liabilities were derecognized as of December 31, 2023. Therefore, if First Mint\u2019s Nevada facilities expand, their PPE would increase and appear in \"Other,\" while derecognized mine assets would not be reflected on the balance sheet, demonstrating a shift from derecognition of sold mines to increased PPE from ongoing Nevada operations.", "context": ["(3) Included in \"Other\" is property, plant and equipment of $3.1 million (2023 51.8 million) for First Mint which includes the Company's bullion store and its minting facility located in Nevada\n\nProperty, plant and equipment, including land and buildings, machinery and equipment; assets under construction and other assets above are allocated by mine as follow:\n\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\n<parameter name=\"file_path\">/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-jmanuelc87-Documents-Companies-Cognitactix-grupo-mexico-evals/dde52c23-b3a0-4425-a3ab-62404ca21536/images/1.png</parameter>\n</invoke>\nalt\n\n\n<invoke name=\"Read\">\n<